{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.898e685814","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-367","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Wexford Observatory is in southern New Zealand. It began operating in 1914.</li><li>Its main telescope's mirror diameter is 11 meters.</li><li>The Orla Observatory is in eastern Poland. It began operating in 1942.</li><li>Its main telescope's mirror diameter is 9 meters.</li><li>Both are observatories located on remote mountaintops.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast the locations of the two observatorys.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Wexford Observatory, which is in southern New Zealand, is one of several observatories located on remote mountaintops.","The Wexford Observatory began operating in 1914, while the Orla Observatory began operating in 1942.","The main telescope's mirror diameter of the Wexford Observatory (11 meters) is greater than that of the Orla Observatory (9 meters).","The Wexford Observatory is in southern New Zealand, and the Orla Observatory is in eastern Poland."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It names where both are and contrasts them. The other choices compare dates or size, or mention only one location."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.ab5e5f2b6e","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-371","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Orla National Park is in central Canada. It was established in 1990.</li><li>Its area is 1,115 square kilometers.</li><li>Umber National Park is in western Kenya. It was established in 1930.</li><li>Its area is 206 square kilometers.</li><li>Both are parks that protect coastal wetlands.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize how the area of the two parks differs.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Both Orla National Park and Umber National Park are parks that protect coastal wetlands.","Orla National Park was established in 1990, while Umber National Park was established in 1930.","Orla National Park, which is in central Canada, is one of several parks that protect coastal wetlands.","The area of Orla National Park (1,115 square kilometers) is greater than that of Umber National Park (206 square kilometers)."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It compares the area of both parks. The other choices describe a similarity, a difference in date, or only one park."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.7441f93e90","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-380","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Orla Observatory is in northern India. It began operating in 1966.</li><li>Its main telescope's mirror diameter is 10 meters.</li><li>The Cresthill Observatory is in eastern Australia. It began operating in 2010.</li><li>Its main telescope's mirror diameter is 7 meters.</li><li>Both are radio observatories.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast the locations of the two observatorys.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The main telescope's mirror diameter of the Orla Observatory (10 meters) is greater than that of the Cresthill Observatory (7 meters).","The Orla Observatory is in northern India, and the Cresthill Observatory is in eastern Australia.","The Orla Observatory, which is in northern India, is one of several radio observatories.","The Orla Observatory began operating in 1966, while the Cresthill Observatory began operating in 2010."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It names where both are and contrasts them. The other choices compare dates or size, or mention only one location."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.fc5d377c38","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-381","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Merrow National Park is in central Canada. It was established in 1970.</li><li>Its area is 6,494 square kilometers.</li><li>Brookhaven National Park is in western Scotland. It was established in 1901.</li><li>Its area is 4,226 square kilometers.</li><li>Both are parks that protect coastal wetlands.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two parks were established.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The area of Merrow National Park (6,494 square kilometers) is greater than that of Brookhaven National Park (4,226 square kilometers).","Merrow National Park is in central Canada, and Brookhaven National Park is in western Scotland.","Both Merrow National Park and Brookhaven National Park are parks that protect coastal wetlands.","Merrow National Park was established in 1970, while Brookhaven National Park was established in 1901."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.8b06d1a112","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-383","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Mount Eastwick is in northern Chile. It was first climbed in 1927.</li><li>Its height is 6,300 meters.</li><li>Mount Juniper is in northern Japan. It was first climbed in 2015.</li><li>Its height is 3,816 meters.</li><li>Both are peaks in the same mountain range.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast the locations of the two mountains.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The height of Mount Eastwick (6,300 meters) is greater than that of Mount Juniper (3,816 meters).","Mount Eastwick was first climbed in 1927, while Mount Juniper was first climbed in 2015.","Mount Eastwick, which is in northern Chile, is one of several peaks in the same mountain range.","Mount Eastwick is in northern Chile, and Mount Juniper is in northern Japan."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It names where both are and contrasts them. The other choices compare dates or size, or mention only one location."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.a8891dd33a","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-390","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Valewood Tunnel is in southern Norway. It opened in 1999.</li><li>Its length is 11 kilometers.</li><li>The Eastwick Tunnel is in southern New Zealand. It opened in 2011.</li><li>Its length is 5 kilometers.</li><li>Both are railway tunnels.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two tunnels opened.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Valewood Tunnel is in southern Norway, and the Eastwick Tunnel is in southern New Zealand.","The length of the Valewood Tunnel (11 kilometers) is greater than that of the Eastwick Tunnel (5 kilometers).","The Valewood Tunnel opened in 1999, while the Eastwick Tunnel opened in 2011.","Both the Valewood Tunnel and the Eastwick Tunnel are railway tunnels."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.3899bdc4ed","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-393","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Lake Redfern is in eastern Poland. It was first surveyed in 1912.</li><li>Its surface area is 422 square kilometers.</li><li>Lake Northam is in eastern Australia. It was first surveyed in 2003.</li><li>Its surface area is 363 square kilometers.</li><li>Both are reservoirs created by dams.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two lakes were first surveyed.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The surface area of Lake Redfern (422 square kilometers) is greater than that of Lake Northam (363 square kilometers).","Lake Redfern was first surveyed in 1912, while Lake Northam was first surveyed in 2003.","Both Lake Redfern and Lake Northam are reservoirs created by dams.","Lake Redfern is in eastern Poland, and Lake Northam is in eastern Australia."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.59527e85b6","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-22","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Priya Takeda is an agricultural scientist.</li><li>Takeda wanted to find out whether planting clover between rows of corn reduces the need for fertilizer.</li><li>Takeda grew corn with and without clover planted between the rows for three seasons.</li><li>Takeda found that fields with clover produced similar yields using a third less fertilizer.</li><li>Takeda concluded that clover may supply some of the nutrients corn needs.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Takeda grew corn with and without clover planted between the rows for three seasons.","Takeda found that fields with clover produced similar yields using a third less fertilizer.","Takeda concluded that clover may supply some of the nutrients corn needs.","Because fields with clover produced similar yields using a third less fertilizer, Takeda concluded that clover may supply some of the nutrients corn needs."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.83cd355982","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-28","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Ayumi Okafor is a materials scientist.</li><li>Okafor wanted to find out whether a coating made from seaweed can keep fruit fresh longer.</li><li>Okafor coated half of a batch of strawberries with a seaweed-based film and left the rest uncoated.</li><li>Okafor found that coated strawberries stayed fresh for about four days longer.</li><li>Okafor concluded that the coating could help reduce food waste.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Okafor concluded that the coating could help reduce food waste.","Because coated strawberries stayed fresh for about four days longer, Okafor concluded that the coating could help reduce food waste.","To investigate the question, Okafor coated half of a batch of strawberries with a seaweed-based film and left the rest uncoated.","Okafor found that coated strawberries stayed fresh for about four days longer."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.5f60ffd9b8","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-29","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Tomás Varga is a materials scientist.</li><li>Varga wanted to find out whether a coating made from seaweed can keep fruit fresh longer.</li><li>Varga coated half of a batch of strawberries with a seaweed-based film and left the rest uncoated.</li><li>Varga found that coated strawberries stayed fresh for about four days longer.</li><li>Varga concluded that the coating could help reduce food waste.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Varga concluded that the coating could help reduce food waste.","Varga found that coated strawberries stayed fresh for about four days longer.","Materials scientist Tomás Varga set out to learn whether a coating made from seaweed can keep fruit fresh longer.","To investigate the question, Varga coated half of a batch of strawberries with a seaweed-based film and left the rest uncoated."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.c662587f25","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-52","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Diego Duarte is a nutrition researcher.</li><li>Duarte wanted to find out whether eating breakfast affects how much food people eat at lunch.</li><li>Duarte asked volunteers to eat breakfast on some days and skip it on others, then measured their lunch portions.</li><li>Duarte found that lunch portions were about the same on both kinds of days.</li><li>Duarte concluded that skipping breakfast may not lead people to eat more later.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Duarte concluded that skipping breakfast may not lead people to eat more later.","Nutrition researcher Diego Duarte set out to learn whether eating breakfast affects how much food people eat at lunch.","Duarte found that lunch portions were about the same on both kinds of days.","To investigate the question, Duarte asked volunteers to eat breakfast on some days and skip it on others, then measured their lunch portions."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.20eda3d414","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-60","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Kenji Delacroix is an astronomer.</li><li>Delacroix wanted to find out whether a distant star's brightness changes on a regular schedule.</li><li>Delacroix measured the star's brightness every night for eighteen months.</li><li>Delacroix found that the star dimmed slightly about every 42 days.</li><li>Delacroix concluded that an orbiting planet may be passing in front of the star.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Delacroix concluded that an orbiting planet may be passing in front of the star.","Delacroix found that the star dimmed slightly about every 42 days.","Astronomer Kenji Delacroix set out to learn whether a distant star's brightness changes on a regular schedule.","To investigate the question, Delacroix measured the star's brightness every night for eighteen months."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.11a9e108ab","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-61","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Viktor Varga is an astronomer.</li><li>Varga wanted to find out whether a distant star's brightness changes on a regular schedule.</li><li>Varga measured the star's brightness every night for eighteen months.</li><li>Varga found that the star dimmed slightly about every 42 days.</li><li>Varga concluded that an orbiting planet may be passing in front of the star.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Astronomer Viktor Varga set out to learn whether a distant star's brightness changes on a regular schedule.","To investigate the question, Varga measured the star's brightness every night for eighteen months.","Varga found that the star dimmed slightly about every 42 days.","Varga concluded that an orbiting planet may be passing in front of the star."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.be33a7b835","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-62","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Beatriz Halvorsen is an astronomer.</li><li>Halvorsen wanted to find out whether a distant star's brightness changes on a regular schedule.</li><li>Halvorsen measured the star's brightness every night for eighteen months.</li><li>Halvorsen found that the star dimmed slightly about every 42 days.</li><li>Halvorsen concluded that an orbiting planet may be passing in front of the star.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Halvorsen found that the star dimmed slightly about every 42 days.","Halvorsen concluded that an orbiting planet may be passing in front of the star.","To investigate the question, Halvorsen measured the star's brightness every night for eighteen months.","Because the star dimmed slightly about every 42 days, Halvorsen concluded that an orbiting planet may be passing in front of the star."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.1d05236181","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-72","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Ayumi Abara is an economist.</li><li>Abara wanted to find out whether a four-day workweek changes employee productivity.</li><li>Abara tracked output at companies before and after they adopted a four-day week.</li><li>Abara found that output per employee stayed the same or rose slightly.</li><li>Abara concluded that shorter workweeks may not reduce productivity.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Abara tracked output at companies before and after they adopted a four-day week.","Abara concluded that shorter workweeks may not reduce productivity.","Abara found that output per employee stayed the same or rose slightly.","Economist Ayumi Abara set out to learn whether a four-day workweek changes employee productivity."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.55557664d9","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-72","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Ayumi Abara is an economist.</li><li>Abara wanted to find out whether a four-day workweek changes employee productivity.</li><li>Abara tracked output at companies before and after they adopted a four-day week.</li><li>Abara found that output per employee stayed the same or rose slightly.</li><li>Abara concluded that shorter workweeks may not reduce productivity.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Abara concluded that shorter workweeks may not reduce productivity.","Abara found that output per employee stayed the same or rose slightly.","To investigate the question, Abara tracked output at companies before and after they adopted a four-day week.","Because output per employee stayed the same or rose slightly, Abara concluded that shorter workweeks may not reduce productivity."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.b2e61c4926","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-75","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Tomás Varga is an economist.</li><li>Varga wanted to find out whether a four-day workweek changes employee productivity.</li><li>Varga tracked output at companies before and after they adopted a four-day week.</li><li>Varga found that output per employee stayed the same or rose slightly.</li><li>Varga concluded that shorter workweeks may not reduce productivity.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Varga found that output per employee stayed the same or rose slightly.","Economist Tomás Varga set out to learn whether a four-day workweek changes employee productivity.","Varga concluded that shorter workweeks may not reduce productivity.","To investigate the question, Varga tracked output at companies before and after they adopted a four-day week."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.d3d5391eac","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-80","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Kenji Varga is a botanist.</li><li>Varga wanted to find out whether tomato plants grow better with music played nearby.</li><li>Varga grew tomato plants in two identical greenhouses, one with music and one without.</li><li>Varga found that plants in both greenhouses grew at similar rates.</li><li>Varga concluded that music may have little effect on tomato growth.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Varga concluded that music may have little effect on tomato growth.","Botanist Kenji Varga set out to learn whether tomato plants grow better with music played nearby.","Varga found that plants in both greenhouses grew at similar rates.","To investigate the question, Varga grew tomato plants in two identical greenhouses, one with music and one without."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.539fd9b4c7","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-100","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Lucía Sato is an education researcher.</li><li>Sato wanted to find out whether reading aloud to young children improves their vocabulary.</li><li>Sato had parents in one group read aloud daily while another group continued their usual routines.</li><li>Sato found that children in the read-aloud group learned more new words.</li><li>Sato concluded that regular read-aloud time may support vocabulary growth.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Sato concluded that regular read-aloud time may support vocabulary growth.","Education researcher Lucía Sato set out to learn whether reading aloud to young children improves their vocabulary.","Sato found that children in the read-aloud group learned more new words.","To investigate the question, Sato had parents in one group read aloud daily while another group continued their usual routines."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.974825c4ca","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-120","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Ravi Vance is an ornithologist.</li><li>Vance wanted to find out whether birds that migrate farther have longer wings.</li><li>Vance measured the wings of several related species with different migration distances.</li><li>Vance found that species that migrated farther tended to have longer, narrower wings.</li><li>Vance concluded that wing shape may be linked to migration distance.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Ornithologist Ravi Vance set out to learn whether birds that migrate farther have longer wings.","Vance found that species that migrated farther tended to have longer, narrower wings.","To investigate the question, Vance measured the wings of several related species with different migration distances.","Vance concluded that wing shape may be linked to migration distance."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.d0611ca18d","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-135","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Viktor Solberg is an archaeologist.</li><li>Solberg wanted to find out whether a coastal settlement traded with inland villages.</li><li>Solberg analyzed the minerals in pottery found at the settlement.</li><li>Solberg found that many pots were made from clay found only far inland.</li><li>Solberg concluded that the settlement may have traded with inland communities.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Archaeologist Viktor Solberg set out to learn whether a coastal settlement traded with inland villages.","Solberg found that many pots were made from clay found only far inland.","Solberg concluded that the settlement may have traded with inland communities.","To investigate the question, Solberg analyzed the minerals in pottery found at the settlement."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.be90a1aae0","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-143","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Tomás Castellano is a neuroscientist.</li><li>Castellano wanted to find out whether learning to juggle changes brain structure.</li><li>Castellano scanned volunteers' brains before and after three months of juggling practice.</li><li>Castellano found that regions linked to tracking movement grew slightly larger.</li><li>Castellano concluded that practicing a new skill may reshape parts of the brain.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Neuroscientist Tomás Castellano set out to learn whether learning to juggle changes brain structure.","Castellano concluded that practicing a new skill may reshape parts of the brain.","Castellano found that regions linked to tracking movement grew slightly larger.","To investigate the question, Castellano scanned volunteers' brains before and after three months of juggling practice."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.ff0b1c954d","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-144","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Ravi Castellano is a marine ecologist.</li><li>Castellano wanted to find out whether sea urchins limit the growth of kelp forests.</li><li>Castellano fenced urchins out of some kelp plots and left others open.</li><li>Castellano found that kelp in the fenced plots grew much denser.</li><li>Castellano concluded that urchins may keep kelp forests from expanding.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Castellano concluded that urchins may keep kelp forests from expanding.","Marine ecologist Ravi Castellano set out to learn whether sea urchins limit the growth of kelp forests.","To investigate the question, Castellano fenced urchins out of some kelp plots and left others open.","Castellano found that kelp in the fenced plots grew much denser."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.4eb03bb05b","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-147","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Amara Haddad is a marine ecologist.</li><li>Haddad wanted to find out whether sea urchins limit the growth of kelp forests.</li><li>Haddad fenced urchins out of some kelp plots and left others open.</li><li>Haddad found that kelp in the fenced plots grew much denser.</li><li>Haddad concluded that urchins may keep kelp forests from expanding.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Haddad found that kelp in the fenced plots grew much denser.","To investigate the question, Haddad fenced urchins out of some kelp plots and left others open.","Marine ecologist Amara Haddad set out to learn whether sea urchins limit the growth of kelp forests.","Haddad concluded that urchins may keep kelp forests from expanding."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.c86854eea0","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-151","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Chen Duarte is a climate scientist.</li><li>Duarte wanted to find out whether light-colored roofs reduce indoor temperatures.</li><li>Duarte compared indoor temperatures in homes with white roofs and homes with dark roofs.</li><li>Duarte found that homes with white roofs stayed several degrees cooler.</li><li>Duarte concluded that light-colored roofs may reduce the need for air-conditioning.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Climate scientist Chen Duarte set out to learn whether light-colored roofs reduce indoor temperatures.","To investigate the question, Duarte compared indoor temperatures in homes with white roofs and homes with dark roofs.","Duarte concluded that light-colored roofs may reduce the need for air-conditioning.","Duarte found that homes with white roofs stayed several degrees cooler."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.2db223c9e8","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-155","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Ravi Okafor is a climate scientist.</li><li>Okafor wanted to find out whether light-colored roofs reduce indoor temperatures.</li><li>Okafor compared indoor temperatures in homes with white roofs and homes with dark roofs.</li><li>Okafor found that homes with white roofs stayed several degrees cooler.</li><li>Okafor concluded that light-colored roofs may reduce the need for air-conditioning.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Okafor concluded that light-colored roofs may reduce the need for air-conditioning.","Okafor found that homes with white roofs stayed several degrees cooler.","To investigate the question, Okafor compared indoor temperatures in homes with white roofs and homes with dark roofs.","Climate scientist Ravi Okafor set out to learn whether light-colored roofs reduce indoor temperatures."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.314ba188fe","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-167","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Priya Castellano is a veterinary scientist.</li><li>Castellano wanted to find out whether dogs recognize their owners' voices.</li><li>Castellano played recordings of owners' voices and strangers' voices to dogs.</li><li>Castellano found that dogs turned toward their owners' voices far more often.</li><li>Castellano concluded that dogs may be able to tell their owners' voices apart from others.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Castellano concluded that dogs may be able to tell their owners' voices apart from others.","Veterinary scientist Priya Castellano set out to learn whether dogs recognize their owners' voices.","Castellano found that dogs turned toward their owners' voices far more often.","To investigate the question, Castellano played recordings of owners' voices and strangers' voices to dogs."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.bebaee15d7","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-178","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Ingrid Mensah is a music researcher.</li><li>Mensah wanted to find out whether learning an instrument improves children's listening skills.</li><li>Mensah tested children's ability to pick out sounds in noise before and after two years of music lessons.</li><li>Mensah found that children who took lessons improved more than those who did not.</li><li>Mensah concluded that music training may sharpen listening skills.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Music researcher Ingrid Mensah set out to learn whether learning an instrument improves children's listening skills.","Mensah found that children who took lessons improved more than those who did not.","To investigate the question, Mensah tested children's ability to pick out sounds in noise before and after two years of music lessons.","Mensah concluded that music training may sharpen listening skills."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."}
